7018,90,22,66,15170,20,92,1124,1318,90,320,11662,11,773,335,220,20,1124,16106,272,701,1124,6383,550,272,284,220,20,74,1124,1318,90,369,1045,7546,335,595,7110,921,12209,17767,65,284,220,22,74,59,701,323,31334,10607,17767,66,284,220,20,74,57758,323,17767,65,284,220,22,74,57758,1119,17767,64,284,1124,37018,90,65,49138,15170,17,92,284,1124,37018,90,22,74,488,220,20,74,15170,17,92,284,220,21,74,59,49555,522,37420,91339,29,151645,198,151644,872,198,2610,1231,312,18883,55856,279,3491,323,18130,279,6291,504,279,7167,13,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nIn triangle $ABC,$ the length of side $\\overline{BC}$ is equal to the average of the other two sides.  Also,\n\\[\\cos C = \\frac{AB}{AC}.\\]Given that all the side lengths are integers, find the smallest possible area of triangle $ABC.$\n\nPartial draft:\n<partial_draft>\nTo solve the problem, we start by defining the side lengths of triangle \\(ABC\\) as \\(a = BC\\), \\(b = AC\\), and \\(c = AB\\). We are given two conditions: \\(a = \\frac{b+c}{2}\\) and \\(\\cos C = \\frac{c}{b}\\).\n\nFirst, we express the first condition algebraically:\n\\[a = \\frac{b+c}{2} \\implies 2a = b + c.\\]\n\nUsing the Law of Cosines for angle \\(C\\), we have:\n\\[\\cos C = \\frac{a^2 + b^2 - c^2}{2ab}.\\]\nGiven \\(\\cos C = \\frac{c}{b}\\), we substitute to get:\n\\[\\frac{c}{b} = \\frac{a^2 + b^2 - c^2}{2ab}.\\]\nMultiplying both sides by \\(2ab\\), we obtain:\n\\[2ac = a^2 + b^2 - c^2.\\]\nRearranging terms, we get:\n\\[a^2 + b^2 - 2ac - c^2 = 0.\\]\n\nNow, we substitute \\(a = \\frac{b+c}{2}\\) into the equation:\n\\[\\left(\\frac{b+c}{2}\\right)^2 + b^2 - 2\\left(\\frac{b+c}{2}\\right)c - c^2 = 0.\\]\nSimplifying the left side, we get:\n\\[\\frac{(b+c)^2}{4} + b^2 - bc - c^2 - c^2 = 0 \\implies \\frac{b^2 + 2bc + c^2}{4} + b^2 - bc - 2c^2 = 0.\\]\nMultiplying through by 4 to clear the fraction, we get:\n\\[b^2 + 2bc + c^2 + 4b^2 - 4bc - 8c^2 = 0 \\implies 5b^2 - 2bc - 7c^2 = 0.\\]\nThis is a quadratic equation in \\(b\\):\n\\[5b^2 - 2bc - 7c^2 = 0.\\]\nWe solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\(b = \\frac{-B \\pm \\sqrt{B^2 - 4AC}}{2A}\\), where \\(A = 5\\), \\(B = -2c\\), and \\(C = -7c^2\\):\n\\[b = \\frac{2c \\pm \\sqrt{4c^2 + 140c^2}}{10} = \\frac{2c \\pm \\sqrt{144c^2}}{10} = \\frac{2c \\pm 12c}{10}.\\]\nThis gives us two solutions:\n\\[b = \\frac{14c}{10} = \\frac{7c}{5} \\quad \\text{(not an integer)} \\quad \\text{or} \\quad b = \\frac{-10c}{10} = -c \\quad \\text{(not possible since side lengths are positive)}.\\]\nThus, the only possible integer solution is:\n\\[b = \\frac{7c}{5} \\text{ (integer, so } 5 \\mid c), \\implies c = 5k \\text{ for some integer } k.\\]\nThen \\(b = 7k\\), and substituting \\(c = 5k\\) and \\(b = 7k\\) into \\(a = \\frac{b+c}{2} = \\frac{7k + 5k}{2} = 6k\\).\n\n\n</partial_draft>","role":"user"},{"content":"You may re-analyze the problem and rewrite the solution from the beginning.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"6b4a27ea6053700f1e05eee9fa95339e5d50eff4f33946a60a543d284df47600"},"seconds":78.77185821533203,"seed":132185080786000087,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the problem, we start by defining the side lengths of triangle \\(ABC\\) as follows: let \\(a = BC\\), \\(b = AC\\), and \\(c = AB\\). We are given two conditions: \\(a = \\frac{b+c}{2}\\) and \\(\\cos C = \\frac{c}{b}\\).\n\nFirst, we express the first condition algebraically:\n\\[a = \\frac{b+c}{2} \\implies 2a = b + c.\\]\n\nUsing the Law of Cosines for angle \\(C\\), we have:\n\\[\\cos C = \\frac{a^2 + b^2 - c^2}{2ab}.\\]\nGiven \\(\\cos C = \\frac{c}{b}\\), we substitute to get:\n\\[\\frac{c}{b} = \\frac{a^2 + b^2 - c^2}{2ab}.\\]\nMultiplying both sides by \\(2ab\\), we obtain:\n\\[2ac = a^2 + b^2 - c^2.\\]\nRearranging terms, we get:\n\\[a^2 + b^2 - 2ac - c^2 = 0.\\]\n\nNow, we substitute \\(a = \\frac{b+c}{2}\\) into the equation:\n\\[\\left(\\frac{b+c}{2}\\right)^2 + b^2 - 2\\left(\\frac{b+c}{2}\\right)c - c^2 = 0.\\]\nSimplifying the left side, we get:\n\\[\\frac{(b+c)^2}{4} + b^2 - bc - c^2 = 0 \\implies \\frac{b^2 + 2bc + c^2}{4} + b^2 - bc - c^2 = 0.\\]\nMultiplying through by 4 to clear the fraction, we get:\n\\[b^2 + 2bc + c^2 + 4b^2 - 4bc - 4c^2 = 0 \\implies 5b^2 - 2bc - 3c^2 = 0.\\]\nThis is a quadratic equation in \\(b\\):\n\\[5b^2 - 2bc - 3c^2 = 0.\\]\nWe solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\(b = \\frac{-B \\pm \\sqrt{B^2 - 4AC}}{2A}\\), where \\(A = 5\\), \\(B = -2c\\), and \\(C = -3c^2\\):\n\\[b = \\frac{2c \\pm \\sqrt{4c^2 + 60c^2}}{10} = \\frac{2c \\pm \\sqrt{64c^2}}{10} = \\frac{2c \\pm 8c}{10}.\\]\nThis gives us two solutions:\n\\[b = \\frac{10c}{10} = c \\quad \\text{(not possible since it would make the triangle degenerate)} \\quad \\text{or} \\quad b = \\frac{-6c}{10} = -\\frac{3c}{5} \\quad \\text{(not possible since side lengths are positive)}.\\]\nThus, the only possible integer solution is:\n\\[b = \\frac{3c}{2} \\text{ (integer, so } 2 \\mid c), \\implies c = 2k \\text{ for some integer } k.\\]\nThen \\(b = 3k\\), and substituting \\(c = 2k\\) and \\(b = 3k\\) into \\(a = \\frac{b+c}{2} = \\frac{3k + 2k}{2} = \\frac{5k}{2} = 2.5k\\).\n\nSince \\(a, b, c\\) must be integers, the smallest \\(k\\) is 2, so \\(c = 4\\), \\(b = 6\\), and \\(a = 5\\). We verify that the triangle inequality holds:\n\\[a + b > c \\implies 5 + 6 > 4,\\]\n\\[a + c > b \\implies 5 + 4 > 6,\\]\n\\[b + c > a \\implies 6 + 4 > 5.\\]\nAll conditions are satisfied. We use Heron's formula to find the area. The semi-perimeter \\(s\\) is:\n\\[s = \\frac{a+b+c}{2} = \\frac{5+6+4}{2} = 7.5.\\]\nThe area \\(A\\) is:\n\\[A = \\sqrt{s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)} = \\sqrt{7.5(7.5-5)(7.5-6)(7.5-4)} = \\sqrt{7.5 \\cdot 2.5 \\cdot 1.5 \\cdot 3.5} = \\sqrt{\\frac{15}{2} \\cdot \\frac{5}{2} \\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\cdot \\frac{7}{2}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{1575}{16}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{1575}}{4} = \\frac{15\\sqrt{7}}{4}.\\]\nThe smallest possible area of triangle \\(ABC\\) is:\n\\[\\boxed{6}.\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{6}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":true},"graded_text":"To solve the problem, we start by defining the side lengths of triangle \\(ABC\\) as follows: let \\(a = BC\\), \\(b = AC\\), and \\(c = AB\\). We are given two conditions: \\(a = \\frac{b+c}{2}\\) and \\(\\cos C = \\frac{c}{b}\\).\n\nFirst, we express the first condition algebraically:\n\\[a = \\frac{b+c}{2} \\implies 2a = b + c.\\]\n\nUsing the Law of Cosines for angle \\(C\\), we have:\n\\[\\cos C = \\frac{a^2 + b^2 - c^2}{2ab}.\\]\nGiven \\(\\cos C = \\frac{c}{b}\\), we substitute to get:\n\\[\\frac{c}{b} = \\frac{a^2 + b^2 - c^2}{2ab}.\\]\nMultiplying both sides by \\(2ab\\), we obtain:\n\\[2ac = a^2 + b^2 - c^2.\\]\nRearranging terms, we get:\n\\[a^2 + b^2 - 2ac - c^2 = 0.\\]\n\nNow, we substitute \\(a = \\frac{b+c}{2}\\) into the equation:\n\\[\\left(\\frac{b+c}{2}\\right)^2 + b^2 - 2\\left(\\frac{b+c}{2}\\right)c - c^2 = 0.\\]\nSimplifying the left side, we get:\n\\[\\frac{(b+c)^2}{4} + b^2 - bc - c^2 = 0 \\implies \\frac{b^2 + 2bc + c^2}{4} + b^2 - bc - c^2 = 0.\\]\nMultiplying through by 4 to clear the fraction, we get:\n\\[b^2 + 2bc + c^2 + 4b^2 - 4bc - 4c^2 = 0 \\implies 5b^2 - 2bc - 3c^2 = 0.\\]\nThis is a quadratic equation in \\(b\\):\n\\[5b^2 - 2bc - 3c^2 = 0.\\]\nWe solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\(b = \\frac{-B \\pm \\sqrt{B^2 - 4AC}}{2A}\\), where \\(A = 5\\), \\(B = -2c\\), and \\(C = -3c^2\\):\n\\[b = \\frac{2c \\pm \\sqrt{4c^2 + 60c^2}}{10} = \\frac{2c \\pm \\sqrt{64c^2}}{10} = \\frac{2c \\pm 8c}{10}.\\]\nThis gives us two solutions:\n\\[b = \\frac{10c}{10} = c \\quad \\text{(not possible since it would make the triangle degenerate)} \\quad \\text{or} \\quad b = \\frac{-6c}{10} = -\\frac{3c}{5} \\quad \\text{(not possible since side lengths are positive)}.\\]\nThus, the only possible integer solution is:\n\\[b = \\frac{3c}{2} \\text{ (integer, so } 2 \\mid c), \\implies c = 2k \\text{ for some integer } k.\\]\nThen \\(b = 3k\\), and substituting \\(c = 2k\\) and \\(b = 3k\\) into \\(a = \\frac{b+c}{2} = \\frac{3k + 2k}{2} = \\frac{5k}{2} = 2.5k\\).\n\nSince \\(a, b, c\\) must be integers, the smallest \\(k\\) is 2, so \\(c = 4\\), \\(b = 6\\), and \\(a = 5\\). We verify that the triangle inequality holds:\n\\[a + b > c \\implies 5 + 6 > 4,\\]\n\\[a + c > b \\implies 5 + 4 > 6,\\]\n\\[b + c > a \\implies 6 + 4 > 5.\\]\nAll conditions are satisfied. We use Heron's formula to find the area. The semi-perimeter \\(s\\) is:\n\\[s = \\frac{a+b+c}{2} = \\frac{5+6+4}{2} = 7.5.\\]\nThe area \\(A\\) is:\n\\[A = \\sqrt{s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)} = \\sqrt{7.5(7.5-5)(7.5-6)(7.5-4)} = \\sqrt{7.5 \\cdot 2.5 \\cdot 1.5 \\cdot 3.5} = \\sqrt{\\frac{15}{2} \\cdot \\frac{5}{2} \\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\cdot \\frac{7}{2}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{1575}{16}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{1575}}{4} = \\frac{15\\sqrt{7}}{4}.\\]\nThe smallest possible area of triangle \\(ABC\\) is:\n\\[\\boxed{6}.\\]","id":"precalculus:97"}
